<node id="Property Law">
  <data key="d0">Property Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property law encompasses the rules governing possessory interests in property, focusing on the objects and subjects of property rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Western Legal Systems">
  <data key="d0">Western Legal Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Western legal systems provide a framework for understanding ownership and possession of property, highlighting the relationship between objects and subjects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anglo-American Law">
  <data key="d0">Anglo-American Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Anglo-American law refers to the legal system that combines English common law with the legal principles of the United States, emphasizing procedural protections of property interests.&lt;SEP&gt;Anglo-American Law generally recognizes multiple possessory rights in land, differing from other legal systems regarding property rights.&lt;SEP&gt;Anglo-American law permits the temporal division of the right of possession, allowing different individuals to hold rights at different times.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4571328bad55741ff2b9b83c0811de82</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil Law">
  <data key="d0">Civil Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Civil law is a legal system based on written codes and statutes, often contrasting with common law systems like Anglo-American law in property definitions and protections.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil Law is characterized by a reluctance to recognize possessory interests in intangible goods compared to Anglo-American Law.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil law is a legal system that also recognizes distinctions between movable and immovable property, similar to Anglo-American law.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil law is a legal system based on written statutes that generally does not recognize divisions of ownership in property.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil law systems typically do not allow for the temporal division of possession rights, focusing instead on the connection between possession and ownership.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil Law refers to a legal system that does not have a direct equivalent to the Anglo-American trust arrangement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d897550811fb362b11f30d05a77830b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4571328bad55741ff2b9b83c0811de82</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water">
  <data key="d0">Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Water is recognized as an essential resource within property law, with special regulations governing its ownership and use.&lt;SEP&gt;Water is treated differently from other types of property in the West, with significant legal implications regarding ownership and usage.&lt;SEP&gt;Water is a tangible thing that is removed from private ownership and is generally regarded as belonging to the state or community.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4571328bad55741ff2b9b83c0811de82</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Land">
  <data key="d0">Land</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Land includes all terrestrial substances and supports property rights, particularly concerning ownership and use adjacent to water bodies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="United States">
  <data key="d0">United States</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States features a unique legal framework in property law, especially regarding the constitutional protection of property interests.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States has established a developed legal framework concerning the ownership and regulation of water resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States has undergone legal reforms in landlord-tenant law driven by judicial action and subsequent legislation, particularly in the 1960s and 1970s.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7caf9193074699e0691d3250604eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4571328bad55741ff2b9b83c0811de82</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Possessory Interests">
  <data key="d0">Possessory Interests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Possessory interests refer to the rights that individuals or entities have concerning the possession of property, encompassing various classifications and ownership types.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eminent Domain">
  <data key="d0">Eminent Domain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Eminent domain is the legal principle that allows a government to take private property for public use, providing compensation to the owner.&lt;SEP&gt;Eminent domain is the power of the state to acquire ownership of privately owned assets, usually with compensation to the owner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ownership Types">
  <data key="d0">Ownership Types</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ownership types categorize the various forms of property ownership, including single individuals, groups, and corporate owners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marital Owners">
  <data key="d0">Marital Owners</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Marital owners refer to partners in a marriage who jointly hold property rights and interests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trusts">
  <data key="d0">Trusts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Trusts are legal arrangements where property is managed by one party for the benefit of another, involving distinct ownership rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zoning and Planning">
  <data key="d0">Zoning and Planning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Zoning and planning refer to regulatory measures that determine land use and development, impacting property rights and ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d4d49bb556e54eabfebc535ba344b2</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Owner of the House">
  <data key="d0">Owner of the House</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The owner of the house has legal rights over the property but may not have the right of occupancy if it is held by another person.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="House Occupant">
  <data key="d0">House Occupant</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The house occupant has exclusive rights of occupancy, which may differ from the ownership rights of the house's owner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adverse Possession">
  <data key="d0">Adverse Possession</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adverse possession allows an individual who occupies land without the owner's permission to eventually claim ownership if certain conditions are met.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statute of Limitations">
  <data key="d0">Statute of Limitations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The statute of limitations sets a maximum time after an event within which legal proceedings may be initiated, affecting ownership claims.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peaceable Possessor">
  <data key="d0">Peaceable Possessor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The peaceable possessor of land has rights against all claims except those of individuals who can prove a superior right to possession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescription">
  <data key="d0">Prescription</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescription is a process in civil law where a possessor acquires title through continuous possession over a certain period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Regulation of Land Use">
  <data key="d0">Public Regulation of Land Use</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Public regulation refers to legal frameworks that govern land use and ownership divisions, potentially affecting property access.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Divisions of Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Divisions of Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical divisions in ownership refer to the ownership of different rights such as mineral rights and air rights, which can complicate property ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cuius Est Solum Eius Est Usque Ad Coelum Et Usque Ad Inferos">
  <data key="d0">Cuius Est Solum Eius Est Usque Ad Coelum Et Usque Ad Inferos</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">This legal maxim implies that land ownership extends vertically to the sky and down to the center of the earth, influencing ownership interpretations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Condominium Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Condominium Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Condominium ownership involves owning a section of property along with shared rights to common areas and utilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cooperative Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Cooperative Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cooperative ownership allows multiple owners to share ownership interests in a single property through a corporation structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Life Estate">
  <data key="d0">Life Estate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Life estate is a legal term describing an interest in property that lasts for the life of a specified individual, after which the interest passes to another entity.&lt;SEP&gt;A life estate grants an individual the right to possess property for their life, with limitations on property use that may affect its value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Remainderman">
  <data key="d0">Remainderman</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The remainderman holds a future interest in property that begins upon the death of the life tenant, with rights to sue for waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reversion">
  <data key="d0">Reversion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reversion refers to the original owner's retained interest in property after granting a life estate to another party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temporal Divisions">
  <data key="d0">Temporal Divisions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Temporal divisions refer to the legal ability to separate the right of possession from ownership, allowing different individuals to have rights at different times.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common-Law Dower">
  <data key="d0">Common-Law Dower</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common-law dower is a legal concept related to the interests of a surviving spouse in the property of the deceased.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curtesy">
  <data key="d0">Curtesy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Curtesy is a legal right in some jurisdictions that allows a husband to benefit from the property of his deceased wife.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8693f4b56d6befe03f991557d9ba5f11</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="O-A Deed">
  <data key="d0">O-A Deed</data>
  <data key="d1">document</data>
  <data key="d2">The O-A Deed is a legal instrument that provides protection for the party B against party A in property transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Debtor">
  <data key="d0">Debtor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Debtor is an individual who owes a debt and may have property rights affected by financial obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Debtor is a person who has the right to possession and use of property unless they default on their obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Creditor">
  <data key="d0">Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Creditor is an individual or institution that lends money and has certain rights over the debtor's property.&lt;SEP&gt;The Creditor is a person or entity that holds a mortgage deed, claiming ownership of the property despite the debtor's rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anglo-American System">
  <data key="d0">Anglo-American System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Anglo-American system refers to legal practices and principles based on common law traditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil-Law System">
  <data key="d0">Civil-Law System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The civil-law system is a legal tradition that has arrived at similar property results and practices, contrasting with the Anglo-American approach.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chattel Mortgage">
  <data key="d0">Chattel Mortgage</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Chattel mortgage is a type of security interest allowing the debtor to retain possession of the property while the creditor has rights over it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Sale">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Sale</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Conditional sale is an arrangement where ownership transfers upon fulfillment of certain conditions, typically payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledge">
  <data key="d0">Pledge</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Pledge is a security agreement where the creditor takes possession of the property, while the debtor retains the right to redeem it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pawn">
  <data key="d0">Pawn</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Pawn refers to a transaction where property is given to a creditor as collateral for a loan, with redemption rights for the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Burglary">
  <data key="d0">Burglary</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Burglary is defined as larceny that involves breaking and entering premises to commit theft.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836178</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Robbery">
  <data key="d0">Robbery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Robbery involves larceny accompanied by the use of force or threats to obtain property from another.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Embezzlement">
  <data key="d0">Embezzlement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Embezzlement is the wrongful taking of property by someone who has lawful access to it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Environmental Law">
  <data key="d0">Environmental Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Environmental law encompasses regulations aimed at protecting community property interests from pollution and environmental harm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Regulatory Law">
  <data key="d0">Regulatory Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Regulatory law is a body of laws designed to govern property use and protect it from adverse effects of regulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Property Rights">
  <data key="d0">Property Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property rights are legal rights to own, use, and manage property, which have evolved in both Anglo-American and civil law.&lt;SEP&gt;Property rights refer to the legal entitlements individuals or entities have regarding the use and ownership of property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d897550811fb362b11f30d05a77830b8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Administrative Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Administrative Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Administrative procedure allows individuals to compel compliance with regulations through formal processes rather than private lawsuits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anglo-American Criminal Codes">
  <data key="d0">Anglo-American Criminal Codes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Anglo-American criminal codes encompass legal statutes and definitions related to theft and associated crimes within the Anglo-American legal tradition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil Law Criminal Codes">
  <data key="d0">Civil Law Criminal Codes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Civil law criminal codes refer to the statutes and legal principles governing theft and related offenses in jurisdictions following civil law traditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9acb02c81b27ae4d1eb0770b5778950e</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marital Property">
  <data key="d0">Marital Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Marital property refers to property owned by a couple that may be subject to division during divorce, including community property and separate property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Community Property">
  <data key="d0">Community Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Community property refers to assets acquired by spouses during marriage, shared equally upon dissolution of the marriage or death.&lt;SEP&gt;Community property is the property accumulated during marriage, typically divided equally upon the death of one spouse or divorce.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28deeed300429d7c272441fee592c5f</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Separate Property">
  <data key="d0">Separate Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Separate property consists of property owned individually by one spouse, typically prior to marriage or received as a gift or inheritance.&lt;SEP&gt;Separate property includes assets owned before marriage or received as gifts or inheritance during marriage, which typically remains with the individual owner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28deeed300429d7c272441fee592c5f</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common-Law Property Jurisdictions">
  <data key="d0">Common-Law Property Jurisdictions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common-law property jurisdictions follow a legal system where property division is determined by the judge's discretion during divorce.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil-Law Jurisdictions">
  <data key="d0">Civil-Law Jurisdictions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Civil-law jurisdictions are legal systems that often have specific rules regarding property rights and ownership, differing from common law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State">
  <data key="d0">State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state refers to the governing body that can withdraw certain tangible things from private ownership and may own public assets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Corporations">
  <data key="d0">Corporations</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Corporations are fictitious legal persons created under incorporation statutes that engage in profit-making activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Highways">
  <data key="d0">Public Highways</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Public highways are roads owned by the state or community that are generally open for public use under certain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Parks">
  <data key="d0">Public Parks</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Public parks are recreational areas owned by the state or local government and accessible to the public.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Air">
  <data key="d0">Air</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Air is regarded by modern Western law as belonging to the state or the community and is not subject to private ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wild Animals">
  <data key="d0">Wild Animals</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Wild animals are considered to be tangible things that are typically removed from private ownership by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fictitious Legal Persons">
  <data key="d0">Fictitious Legal Persons</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fictitious legal persons are organizations such as corporations created under incorporation statutes that can engage in various activities, including profit-making.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triad of Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Triad of Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The triad of ownership refers to the owner's rights to possess, use, and dispose of a thing, commonly referenced in civil-law traditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88135481e4f734cf0010325351f4349</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="American Legal System">
  <data key="d0">American Legal System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The American legal system, comprising common-law and civil-law traditions, is characterized by distinct procedures for property rights and ownership actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil-Law Systems">
  <data key="d0">Civil-Law Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Civil-law systems encompass legal systems that differ notably in property ownership practices from common law, particularly regarding co-ownership.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil-law systems are legal frameworks that focus on codified statutes and regulations, differing from common-law in their treatment of ownership and possession actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28deeed300429d7c272441fee592c5f</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Roman Law">
  <data key="d0">Roman Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Roman law laid the foundational principles of legal distinction between personal actions and real property actions, influencing later legal systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ejectment Action">
  <data key="d0">Ejectment Action</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The ejectment action is a common-law procedure used to recover possession of land by demonstrating a better right to possession than the defendant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quiet-Title Action">
  <data key="d0">Quiet-Title Action</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The quiet-title action is an equity procedure aimed at securing judicial recognition of the plaintiff's title to land against opposing claims.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trespass">
  <data key="d0">Trespass</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Trespass refers to an unlawful entry onto someone's property, traditionally resulting in personal actions for damages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Possessory Actions">
  <data key="d0">Possessory Actions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Possessory actions are legal proceedings aimed at recovering physical possession of property instead of claims of ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judicial Declaration">
  <data key="d0">Judicial Declaration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A judicial declaration is a formal judgment regarding the rights or status of parties concerning a legal matter, such as land ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movable Property Actions">
  <data key="d0">Movable Property Actions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Movable property actions deal with ownership rights and the recovery of personal property rather than real estate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="French Revendication">
  <data key="d0">French Revendication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The French revendication is a legal action that allows an individual to recover property through a formal claim, often testing relative ownership rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="German Law">
  <data key="d0">German Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">German law features a co-ownership model known as Gesamthandeigentum, restricting partitioning of tenancy property among co-owners.&lt;SEP&gt;German law includes various legal actions regarding property rights, closely aligning with foundational principles from Roman law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28deeed300429d7c272441fee592c5f</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anglo-American Systems">
  <data key="d0">Anglo-American Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Anglo-American systems refer to the legal frameworks derived from English common law and American law, particularly in relation to property ownership and actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statutory Possessory Action">
  <data key="d0">Statutory Possessory Action</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A statutory possessory action is a legal remedy that allows previous possessors to recover property from those who lack a valid claim of right.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injunction">
  <data key="d0">Injunction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An injunction is a court order to prevent an individual from doing something that interferes with the rights of another party, often used in property disputes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Forcible Entry and Detainer">
  <data key="d0">Forcible Entry and Detainer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Forcible entry and detainer refers to legal actions taken to regain possession of property from someone who has unlawfully retained it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3db55e3cfb6d996646a6223672321b54</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anglo-American Trust">
  <data key="d0">Anglo-American Trust</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Anglo-American Trust is a legal arrangement enabling the passage of property interests across generations without probate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Waqf">
  <data key="d0">Waqf</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Waqf is an Islamic legal institution similar to the Anglo-American trust.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Charitable Trust">
  <data key="d0">Charitable Trust</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Charitable Trust in Anglo-American law serves specific charitable purposes and has an equivalent in civil law foundations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Probate">
  <data key="d0">Probate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Probate is a legal process for transferring property following a person’s death, often cumbersome in Anglo-American jurisdictions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Secured Transaction">
  <data key="d0">Secured Transaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Secured Transaction involves using property as security for loans or obligations, recognized in both Anglo-American and civil law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mortgage is a common law device where property ownership is temporarily transferred to a creditor under certain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypothec">
  <data key="d0">Hypothec</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothec is a civil law pledge that allows a debtor to retain possession of property while using it as security for a debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equity Courts">
  <data key="d0">Equity Courts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equity Courts are judicial entities that protect debtors' rights and ensure fair treatment in property transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Title">
  <data key="d0">Legal Title</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal Title refers to formal ownership rights to property, which a debtor may not possess if encumbered by a mortgage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Parties">
  <data key="d0">Third Parties</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Third Parties are individuals or entities potentially affected by property transactions involving debtors and creditors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f30cc379bb0d23d68692c29ef88ef5de</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Remainder">
  <data key="d0">Remainder</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Remainder is a future interest in property that takes effect after the termination of a prior interest, such as a life estate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Edith">
  <data key="d0">Edith</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Edith is a person mentioned as the beneficiary of a life estate in the example provided, who will possess the property for her life.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="George">
  <data key="d0">George</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">George is mentioned as the son of Edith who is to inherit a life estate after his mother’s death and has children who would inherit after him.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contingencies">
  <data key="d0">Contingencies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Contingencies refer to conditions that must be met for certain interests in property or rights to take effect, as described in various legal contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Usufruct">
  <data key="d0">Usufruct</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Usufruct is a right in civil law that gives a person temporary rights to use and benefit from another's property without owning it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fideicommissary Substitutions">
  <data key="d0">Fideicommissary Substitutions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fideicommissary substitutions are legal mechanisms allowing for conditional gifts in civil law, a remnant of ancient Roman law regarding legacies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tenancy">
  <data key="d0">Tenancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenancy describes a legal agreement in which one party occupies land or property owned by another party, typically articulated through rental agreements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Landlord-Tenant Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Landlord-Tenant Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The landlord-tenant relationship pertains to the legal obligations and rights between landlords and tenants regarding property use and possession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Residential Tenancies">
  <data key="d0">Residential Tenancies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Residential tenancies refer to rental agreements that pertain to living accommodations, often subject to specific laws and regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential tenancies pertain to rental agreements for living accommodations, significantly impacted by recent legal changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7caf9193074699e0691d3250604eeb</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commercial Tenancies">
  <data key="d0">Commercial Tenancies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Commercial tenancies are legal agreements allowing businesses to lease property for commercial purposes under specific negotiated terms.&lt;SEP&gt;Commercial tenancies are rental agreements for business properties, primarily relying on private negotiations and traditional lease forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a7caf9193074699e0691d3250604eeb</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fee Simple">
  <data key="d0">Fee Simple</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fee simple is a type of freehold ownership in property law that provides the most complete form of ownership with the potential for total control of the property, barring certain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fee Simple Determinable">
  <data key="d0">Fee Simple Determinable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fee simple determinable is a form of fee simple ownership that may automatically terminate when a designated condition occurs, such as failure to use the property for specified purposes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fee Simple Subject to a Condition Subsequent">
  <data key="d0">Fee Simple Subject to a Condition Subsequent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fee simple subject to a condition subsequent is a type of fee simple ownership that remains valid unless a specific event occurs that allows the prior owner to reclaim ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-15e6a315e9822d898cf2d6d3debf0ac8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Government Grants">
  <data key="d0">Government Grants</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Government grants refer to monetary support provided by the government that has become more secure in recent years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d897550811fb362b11f30d05a77830b8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Landlord-Tenant Law">
  <data key="d0">Landlord-Tenant Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Landlord-tenant law governs the relationship between landlords and tenants, with a trend toward greater security for tenants in the West.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d897550811fb362b11f30d05a77830b8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anglo-American Property Law">
  <data key="d0">Anglo-American Property Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Anglo-American property law historically distinguished between interests in land and other types of property, with modern shifts toward more similarity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d897550811fb362b11f30d05a77830b8</data>
  <data key="d4">property_law.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1774836180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movable Property">
  <data key="d0">Movable Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Movable property refers to personal possessions that are protected under different legal actions than immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d897550811fb362b11f30d05a77830b8</data>
